WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.040
Hi, I'm Aston Motes, and I was the first software engineer hired at Dropbox.

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:09.960
I also work with an organization called /dev/color.

00:00:09.984 --> 00:00:14.184
/dev/color is a nonprofit that advances the careers of Black software engineers.

00:00:14.184 --> 00:00:18.564
Sprites make it much easier to organize your code, but visually they're still

00:00:18.564 --> 00:00:19.968
just rectangles.

00:00:19.968 --> 00:00:23.968
To make our creations come to life, we'll want to add images to our sprites.

00:00:24.968 --> 00:00:29.953
We'll start with the simple program you've seen before that creates a single rectangular sprite.

00:00:29.953 --> 00:00:36.893
Before we can turn this rectangle into something more fun, we need to tell Game Lab about the

00:00:36.893 --> 00:00:38.193
image we want to use.

00:00:38.193 --> 00:00:39.937
All images and drawings you add to

00:00:39.937 --> 00:00:44.897
your project are created and managed in the Animation tab. To switch back and forth between

00:00:44.897 --> 00:00:49.697
your code and the animations, use a toggle in the upper left corner.

00:00:49.922 --> 00:00:53.122
To add a new animation, click the plus sign.

00:00:53.122 --> 00:00:57.882
This will open a library of images that are already built into Game Lab.

00:00:57.882 --> 00:00:59.907
From the library, you can browse the

00:00:59.907 --> 00:01:04.907
categories to find what you're looking for.

00:01:04.907 --> 00:01:08.907
Once you've selected an image, it'll be added to your project and shown on the left.

00:01:09.891 --> 00:01:12.891
If you like, you can also change the name of your image.

00:01:14.291 --> 00:01:17.791
So, here we've added an image in the Animation tab,

00:01:17.791 --> 00:01:19.791
and we have code that creates a basic sprite.

00:01:19.876 --> 00:01:25.876
Now we'll need to put those two together and tell the computer that we want this particular sprite.

00:01:25.876 --> 00:01:28.876
We'll use the setAnimation block to do this.

00:01:28.876 --> 00:01:37.860
First, we'll use the name of our sprite and the name of the image we selected in the Animation tab.

00:01:38.860 --> 00:01:39.845
Now when we run our program,

00:01:39.845 --> 00:01:42.245
the sprite will be drawn with the image we selected.

00:01:42.245 --> 00:01:46.165
You're not limited to the images that come built into Game Lab.

00:01:46.165 --> 00:01:48.805
You can also choose to upload an image from your computer.

00:01:49.829 --> 00:01:52.829
Or draw something yourself.

00:01:52.829 --> 00:01:58.429
In fact, you can edit any image in the Animation tab using the Pixel Editor.

00:01:58.429 --> 00:01:59.429
Using the different commands in the Pixel Editor,

00:01:59.814 --> 00:02:03.474
you can easily draw, erase, or modify images.

00:02:05.474 --> 00:02:07.354
To adjust the dimensions of an image,

00:02:07.354 --> 00:02:09.799
just make changes in the menu on the right side.

00:02:09.799 --> 00:02:15.719
It's also just one click to flip your image to face the opposite direction.

00:02:15.719 --> 00:02:19.039
If you like an image you've made but want to make some changes, just make a copy.

00:02:19.783 --> 00:02:23.103
There are lots of helpful little tools in the Pixel Editor.

00:02:23.103 --> 00:02:25.703
Play around with them on your own and see what they do.

00:02:25.703 --> 00:02:29.763
Adding images or your own drawings to sprites lets you unleash your creativity

00:02:29.768 --> 00:02:31.968
and make your programs really your own.

00:02:31.968 --> 00:02:32.968
Let's get to it.
